{"name":"Crème","hex":"#ffffb6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crème","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Synonym of cream."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very sugary, fluffy white cream derivative or creamlike analogue.","Cream."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cr%C3%A8me"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cream","blocks":[{"text":"Cream is a dairy product composed of the higher-fat layer skimmed from the top of milk before homogenization. The butterfat, which is less dense, rises to the top and is skimmed off, resulting in gravity cream. The industrial production of cream instead uses centrifugal separators to make separator cream. Cream is often sold in grades defined by their butterfat content. It contains high levels of saturated fat, in ascending proportion from light cream to heavy cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg/330px-01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream","wikidata":"Q13228"},{"title":"Custard","blocks":[{"text":"Custard is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened milk, cheese, or cream cooked with egg or egg yolk to thicken it, and sometimes also flour, corn starch, or gelatin. Depending on the recipe, custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring sauce to the thick pastry cream used to fill éclairs. The most common custards are used in custard desserts or dessert sauces and typically include sugar and vanilla; however, savory custards are also found, e.g., in quiche."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/330px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custard","wikidata":"Q625825"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Milk-